#
# prima-materia — the Vedic solemn ritual, taken down from the register
#
# The instance was registered in traditions/registry-indic.ttl and is moved
# here now that passages have been read. Two of the three items its
# registration names have been opened — the Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa in the English
# of 1882 to 1900 and the Aitareya Brāhmaṇa in the English of 1920 — and the
# third, the Śrautasūtras, has not; the file says so at the work identifiers
# rather than letting the coverage state cover it. The Kauṣītaki Brāhmaṇa was
# opened as well although the registration did not name it: it stands in the
# same translated volume as the Aitareya, and it carries the passage that
# turned out to be the most valuable thing in the whole reading.
#
# Why this one out of the register. The examinability-driven entrance of
# SPEC §14 asks for the transmissions that name terms, cautions and conditions
# of failure. This one is the densest such corpus the register holds. Its
# characteristic sentence is not "do this and that will follow" but "if he
# does not do this, that will befall him", and it has a whole book of the
# form "if such and such goes wrong, what rite and what expiation would there
# be in that case?" — the enumerated mishap with its prescribed repair, some
# twenty-two of them in four chapters.
#
# What the reading found, and it is one thing said three ways. This
# transmission states its failure conditions more plainly than anything else
# in this holding; it also, at every point where a failure could be checked,
# supplies something that keeps it from being. The altar is piled and the gods
# do not become immortal, twice, and the third attempt succeeds by an exact
# count — whereupon the transmission rules that nobody after them will be
# immortal in the body. The fire goes out and the eldest son dies, but only
# for the sacrificer who does not know; the one who knows has prevailed by
# knowing. The spilled milk is a mishap in one chapter and a sign of increase
# in the next sentence. And the expiation, prescribed by case for twenty-two
# cases, is said to work without exception and without sign.
#
# Order of the file: the tradition and what was read; the works; the altar
# piled to a count and the clause that closes the test; what the exact wording
# is said to do and to whom; the catalogue of mishaps at the fire and the
# knowledge that averts them; the expiation and its silence; the capacities
# claimed for knowing; and the dispute the Kauṣītaki carries about whether
# there is a fault at all.
#

pmt:VedicRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "Vedic ritual transmission"@en, "Vedische Ritualüberlieferung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The solemn ritual and its exegesis, in which efficacy rests on stated equivalences between the act, the body and the world."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run at the wording, on 2026-09-19. The Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa in the Mādhyandina recension, in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East volumes 12, 26, 41, 43 and 44, Oxford 1882 to 1900. Read closely: I,6,3 (the misspoken formula and Vṛtra); I,9,2 (the unyoking of the sacrifice); X,4,3 (the count of bricks and immortality); XI,2,7,33 (the balance); XI,5,3 (the dialogue of Śauceya and Uddālaka on what befalls when a fire goes out); XI,5,6,9 (the daily recitation); XII,4,1 to XII,4,4 (the expiations of the Agnihotra). The Aitareya and Kauṣītaki Brāhmaṇas in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920: read closely Aitareya iii,7 to iii,11 (the vaṣaṭ call and the Nivid) and Kauṣītaki xxvi,4 to xxvi,6 (the flaw in the recitation). Everything else in both works was searched but not read: the five Eggeling volumes hold fourteen kāṇḍas and the Keith volume two further Brāhmaṇas entire. This is five passages out of a corpus of some four thousand pages, and no claim of exhaustion is made for it."@en ;
    skos:note "Not opened at all: the Śrautasūtras, which the registration names as the third part of the principal corpus, and which carry the expiation as a book of its own — Kātyāyana 25, Āpastamba 9. Eggeling cites Kātyāyana at exactly the places entered below, so the address is known and the work is not."@en ;
    skos:note "The recension is part of the citation and not a detail. Eggeling translates the Mādhyandina; the Kāṇva recension divides into seventeen kāṇḍas against fourteen and its numbering does not convert, so a Kāṇva place has to be given on its own. No free full translation of the Kāṇva was found in this run, and the Kāṇva readings entered below are those Eggeling reports in his notes and not readings this run has seen."@en ;
    skos:note "What kind of witness this is. The Brāhmaṇas are not a liturgy but the commentary that travels with one: they state what each act is for, what it is the counterpart of, and what happens if it is done wrong. That makes them unusually good evidence for what the tradition claimed and no evidence at all for what was done — the sūtras hold the performance and were not opened. A claim read here is a claim of the exegesis."@en ;
    skos:note "The contact route stays at documented and this run found nothing to move it. What it did find is worth recording beside the value: the nearest formal parallel to this corpus's characteristic claim, that the knower of a formula obtains a named result, is the knowledge rubric of the Egyptian funerary literature, and the nearest parallel to its expiation book is the Mesopotamian namburbû. Neither was opened and no node here rests on either."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa; Aitareya Brāhmaṇa; the Śrautasūtras" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana, pmw:AitareyaBrahmana, pmw:KausitakiBrahmana, pmw:KatyayanaSrautasutra, pmw:ApastambaSrautasutra ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa X,4,3,4-8 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, The Śatapatha-Brāhmaṇa, Sacred Books of the East 43, Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1897, pp. 356-357" .

#
# The works of this corpus as identifiers
#

pmw:SatapathaBrahmana a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa"@en, "Śatapatha-Brāhmaṇa"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-19 in the Mādhyandina recension, in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East volumes 12, 26, 41, 43 and 44, Oxford 1882 to 1900, covering kāṇḍas I to XIV. Five passages were read at the wording; the rest of the fourteen kāṇḍas was searched and not read. The Kāṇva recension is a separate text for citation purposes and was not obtained."@en .

pmw:AitareyaBrahmana a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa"@en, "Aitareya-Brāhmaṇa"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-19 in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas: The Aitareya and Kauṣītaki Brāhmaṇas of the Rigveda, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge, Massachusetts 1920. Read at the wording: the third pañcikā, chapters 7 to 11, on the vaṣaṭ call and the Nivid. The remaining seven pañcikās were searched and not read. Keith gives both the pañcikā count and the continuous adhyāya count in brackets, and the places below give both because the older literature uses the second."@en .

pmw:KausitakiBrahmana a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Kauṣītaki Brāhmaṇa"@en, "Kauṣītaki-Brāhmaṇa"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-19 in the same volume and translation as the Aitareya. Read at the wording: chapter xxvi, sections 4 to 6, which carry the named disagreement about whether a detected flaw in the recitation has to be repaired at all. The registration of this tradition did not name this work; it is added to the corpus at the instance because the reading found it there, and the coined identifier of the tradition is kept unchanged."@en .

pmw:KatyayanaSrautasutra a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Kātyāyana Śrautasūtra"@en, "Kātyāyana-Śrautasūtra"@de ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. Its twenty-fifth adhyāya is the expiation book, and Eggeling's notes to the passages entered below refer to it by section at exactly the points where the Brāhmaṇa states a repair. Whoever opens it can say whether the performance manual carries a sign of a completed expiation where the exegesis carries none. Weber's edition of 1859 is public domain."@en .

pmw:ApastambaSrautasutra a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Āpastamba Śrautasūtra"@en, "Āpastamba-Śrautasūtra"@de ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. Its ninth praśna is the expiation book of the other school. Garbe's edition of 1882 to 1902 is public domain."@en .

#
# The altar piled to a count, and the clause that closes the test
#

pmp:VaidikaAgnicayana a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "Agnicayana, the piling of the fire-altar"@en, "Agnicayana, die Schichtung des Feueraltars"@de ;
    skos:definition "The construction of a brick altar in five layers, treated by the transmission as the building of a body for the year, for death and for the sacrificer at once. The exegesis makes its efficacy depend on the completeness of the count: enclosing-stones for the nights, bricks laid with formulas for the days, half-moons, months and seasons, and plain filling bricks for the hours."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa X,4,3,8-13 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 43, Oxford 1897, pp. 357-358" .

pmc:VaidikaPunarmrtyu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "Punarmṛtyu, the death that comes again"@en, "Punarmṛtyu, der wiederkehrende Tod"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition the transmission offers to remove: not death but its recurrence. Those who neither know nor perform the work come to life again when they have died, and become the food of Death time after time; those who know or perform it come to life again and come to immortal life."@en ;
    skos:note "Eggeling renders the term throughout as recurring or recurrent death and Keith as repeated death, so a search in one wording misses the other half of the corpus. This was the case in the searches carried in this run and is recorded at examinations/searches-vedic.ttl."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa X,4,3,10 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 43, Oxford 1897, pp. 357-358" .

pmc:VaidikaAgnicayanaAmrtatva a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "The piled altar yields escape from the death that comes again"@en, "Der geschichtete Altar bringt das Entkommen vom wiederkehrenden Tod hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:VaidikaAgnicayana ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:VaidikaPunarmrtyu ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    skos:note "Entered as the claim of the transmission. What makes it worth more than the ordinary promise of a rite is that the passage reports two failures of its own before it succeeds, which almost nothing else in this holding does. See pmc:VaidikaIstakasamkhya."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa X,4,3,8-10 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 43, Oxford 1897, pp. 357-358" .

pmc:VaidikaIstakasamkhya a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "The exact count of stones and bricks, and the two failures that establish it"@en, "Die genaue Zahl der Steine und Ziegel, und die zwei Fehlschläge, die sie festsetzen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission states the condition by reporting its own failure twice. The gods perform the Agnihotra, the new and full-moon sacrifices, the seasonal offerings, the animal sacrifice and the Soma-sacrifice: they did not attain immortality. They build a fire-altar with unlimited enclosing-stones and unlimited bricks, as some do to this day saying that the gods did so: they did not attain immortality. Prajāpati then names the fault — ye either make me too large or leave me defective, therefore ye do not become immortal — and the count: three hundred and sixty enclosing-stones, three hundred and sixty bricks laid with formulas and thirty-six more, and ten thousand eight hundred filling bricks. The gods laid down accordingly and thereafter became immortal."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the only place the run found in either work where a defective performance is reported as having actually failed and the fault is then named. It is a report about gods and not about men, which is why it is entered as the ground of the condition and not as a case."@en ;
    skos:note "The numbers are the point and they are checkable in a way almost nothing else here is: a man either laid three hundred and sixty enclosing-stones or he did not. What is not checkable is the outcome attached to them, and the next paragraph of the same passage is where that is settled. See pmc:VaidikaNaSarirenaAmrtah."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:VaidikaAgnicayanaAmrtatva ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:VaidikaSamkhyapurnata ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Ye do not lay down all my forms; but ye either make me too large or leave me defective: therefore ye do not become immortal. Stated by Prajāpati in the passage itself, as the reason for two reported failures."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa X,4,3,4-8 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 43, Oxford 1897, pp. 356-357" .

pmc:VaidikaNaSarirenaAmrtah a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "Henceforward no one shall be immortal with the body"@en, "Von nun an soll niemand mit dem Leibe unsterblich sein"@de ;
    skos:definition "The clause that follows the successful piling, and it is spoken by Death. He objects that on this wise all men will become immortal and asks what share will then be his. The gods answer: henceforward no one shall be immortal with the body; only when Death has taken the body as his share shall he who is to become immortal, either through knowledge or through holy work, become immortal after separating from the body. The passage then identifies both the knowledge and the holy work with this same fire-altar."@en ;
    skos:note "What the sentence does is worth stating exactly, because the transmission does not hide it and a summary would. Three paragraphs earlier the claim had an outcome anyone could look for: the performers of the rite would not die. Here the outcome is moved past the death of the body by a rule stated inside the same passage, and the reason given for moving it is that Death would otherwise have no share. From this point the claim excludes nothing that could be observed of a performer."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the sharpest instance in this holding of a construction that is usually reconstructed rather than read: a transmission that had a checkable promise, met an objection, and narrowed the promise in the same breath. The objector is a character in the story and the narrowing is presented as a concession to him."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa X,4,3,9-10 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 43, Oxford 1897, pp. 357-358" .

#
# What the exact wording is said to do, and to whom
#

pmc:VaidikaIndrasatru a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "Grow thou, having Indra for thy foe"@en, "Wachse, der du Indra zum Feinde hast"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission's own demonstration that the wording of an offering formula decides the outcome. Tvaṣṭṛ, whose son Indra has killed and whose Soma Indra has drunk uninvited, pours what is left into the fire saying: Grow thou, having Indra for thy foe. What grows from the fire is Vṛtra. The passage then draws the lesson in one sentence: because he said Grow thou, having Indra for thy foe, therefore Indra slew him; had he said Grow thou, the foe of Indra, he would certainly have forthwith slain Indra."@en ;
    skos:note "Who bears the fault is the finding. Tvaṣṭṛ speaks and Vṛtra dies. The speaker of the formula loses nothing by his mistake except the thing he produced with it, and the thing produced is what the mistake kills. See generalizations/displaced-penalties.ttl, where this is now a witness."@en ;
    skos:note "What the Śatapatha says and what it does not. It makes the outcome turn on the wording and gives the two wordings; it does not say that the fault lay in the accent. That is supplied by Eggeling's note from the Taittirīya-Saṃhitā II,4,12,1, where the compound is said to have been accented on the first member instead of the second. The Taittirīya was not opened in this run, and the accent is therefore reported here as the translator's note and not as a reading of this holding. The same legend is told again at Śatapatha V,5,4,2 and following, which this run did not read."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:VaidikaDarsapurnamasa ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa I,6,3,8-10 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 12, Oxford 1882, pp. 165-166" .

pmp:VaidikaVasatkara a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AitareyaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "The vaṣaṭ call"@en, "Der Vaṣaṭ-Ruf"@de ;
    skos:definition "The call with which, at the end of the offering formula, the oblation is poured into the fire. The Aitareya treats it as an instrument in the Hotṛ's hand and distinguishes three: the thunderbolt, made aloud and forcibly, which is hurled at a rival; the hider of his abode, even and continuous and without loss of part of the verse, on which offspring and cattle depend; and the empty, in which the syllable sat fails."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,7 (xi,7), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge, Massachusetts 1920, p. 169" .

pmc:VaidikaVasatkaraRiktah a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AitareyaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "The empty call, in which the syllable fails"@en, "Der leere Ruf, in dem die Silbe ausfällt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The third of the three vaṣaṭ calls and the defective one: that one wherein the sat fails is the empty; he empties himself, he empties the sacrificer; the sayer of vaṣaṭ becomes worse, he becomes worse for whom he says vaṣaṭ. Therefore he should not desire it."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated condition of failure located in a single syllable, with the damage named on both sides: on the reciter and on the man he recites for. The commentator Sāyaṇa, as Keith reports him, takes the loss to lie in a lowered pronunciation, so the fault is one of delivery and not of wording."@en ;
    skos:note "The same chapter makes the call an instrument of deliberate harm and benefit, which is entered separately at pmc:VaidikaSvarenaKarma. Read together, the two say that the same variation which is a fault when it happens is a technique when it is intended."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:VaidikaVasatkara ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,7 (xi,7), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, p. 169" .

pmc:VaidikaVasatkaraSantih a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AitareyaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "The call is a thunderbolt and must be appeased after every use"@en, "Der Ruf ist ein Donnerkeil und ist nach jedem Gebrauch zu besänftigen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The vaṣaṭ call is a thunderbolt; it shines when hurled if not appeased. Of it not every man as it were knows the appeasing nor the support. From it even now there is often as it were death. The appeasing of it, the support, is Speech; therefore after each vaṣaṭ call the Hotṛ should recite as accompaniment: O vaṣaṭ call, do not injure me; let me not injure thee. Appeased, it injures him not."@en ;
    skos:note "Three things stand in this short passage that rarely stand together. A present-tense claim of harm — from it even now there is often as it were death — which is not set in myth and not deferred. An admission that the countermeasure is not generally known. And a countermeasure to be applied after every single use, which is what a transmission does when it treats its own danger as real rather than as emphasis."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter then carries a disagreement about the countermeasure itself: an unnamed authority holds that the long formula is impotent and that Force, strength, force is what should accompany the call. Two remedies for one danger, with the second proposed against the first."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:VaidikaVasatkara ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,8 (xi,8), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, pp. 169-170" .

pmc:VaidikaSvarenaKarma a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AitareyaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "By the pitch of the call the Hotṛ makes the sacrificer better or worse at will"@en, "Durch die Tonhöhe des Rufes macht der Hotṛ den Opferherrn nach Belieben besser oder schlechter"@de ;
    skos:definition "A capacity claimed for the officiant over the man who employs him, with the procedure stated. Should he consider the good and evil of the sacrificer whose Hotṛ he is? He should do to him herein as he may desire. If he desires that the man be as he was before sacrificing, he says the vaṣaṭ call in the same tone as he recites the verse. If he desires that he be worse, he recites the verse in a more raised tone and says the call in a more depressed tone. If he desires that he be better, he recites in a more depressed tone and says the call in a more raised tone."@en ;
    skos:note "This is a capacity in the sense this project looks for and an unusually well specified one: the operation is a relative pitch between two utterances, it is performed in the open in the presence of the person it acts on, and the three intended outcomes are named. What is not named is any sign by which the sacrificer or anyone else would know which of the three was done to him, and the transmission puts the choice in the Hotṛ's discretion without requiring him to declare it."@en ;
    skos:note "Kept apart from pmc:VaidikaVasatkaraRiktah on purpose, although both turn on the delivery of the same call. One is a fault with a consequence, the other a technique with an intention; entering them as one node would have lost the fact that this corpus describes the same variation twice, once as damage and once as instrument."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,7 (xi,7), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, p. 169" .

pmp:VaidikaNivid a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AitareyaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "The recitation of the Nivid"@en, "Die Rezitation der Nivid"@de ;
    skos:definition "The insertion of a short formula of set sentences into a litany — before it at the morning pressing, in the middle at midday, at the end at the third pressing. The Aitareya calls the Nivids the embryos and the ornaments of the litanies, and recites them by sentences because the gods gathered the sacrifice together by quarters."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,10-11 (xi,10-11), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, pp. 171-172" .

pmc:VaidikaNividdosah a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AitareyaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "Three ways of misreciting the Nivid, and three graded consequences"@en, "Drei Weisen, die Nivid zu verfehlen, und drei abgestufte Folgen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission grades the fault and grades the damage with it. He should not pass over a sentence of the Nivid; if he were to pass over one, he would make a break in the sacrifice, and as the break grows the sacrificer becomes worse. He should not invert two sentences; if he were to invert two, he would confuse the sacrifice, and the sacrificer would be confused. He should not unite two sentences; if he were to unite two, he would contract the life of the sacrifice, and the sacrificer would be likely to die. A fourth follows the same form: inserting the Nivid at the third pressing with two verses left over instead of one injures the propagative power and deprives people of embryos."@en ;
    skos:note "Three degrees of a fault in a recitation, three degrees of harm, and in all four cases the harm falls on the sacrificer and not on the reciter who made it. The reciter is the only person in a position to know that he passed over a sentence, and he is the one person named as suffering nothing."@en ;
    skos:note "The grading is what makes this more than a threat. Omission, inversion and fusion are three different things a reciter can do to the same text, and the transmission assigns them consequences of different severity in a fixed order — worse, confused, likely to die. A doctrine that only warned would not need to rank them."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:VaidikaNivid ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,11 (xi,11), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, pp. 171-172" .

#
# The catalogue of mishaps at the fire, and the knowledge that averts them
#

pmp:VaidikaAgnihotra a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "Agnihotra, the daily milk oblation"@en, "Agnihotra, die tägliche Milchspende"@de ;
    skos:definition "The oblation of milk poured into the fire morning and evening, which the transmission calls a sacrificial session entered upon for life and describes as ensuring death in old age. Three fires are kept for it: the offering fire, the householder's fire and the southern cooking fire."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XII,4,1,1 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, p. 177" .

pmc:VaidikaAgnyupasamana a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "What befalls when a fire goes out, fire by fire"@en, "Was eintritt, wenn ein Feuer erlischt, Feuer für Feuer"@de ;
    skos:definition "Śauceya Prācīnayogya puts five cases to Uddālaka Āruṇi in turn and Uddālaka names the damage for each. If the fires are taken out and the vessels brought down and the offering fire then goes out, before long the eldest son would die. If at that moment the householder's fire goes out, before long the master of the house would die. If the southern cooking fire goes out, before long all the cattle would die. If all the fires go out, before long the family would be without heirs. If all the fires go out and there is no wind blowing, unpleasant things would be seen in this world and unpleasant things in the world beyond. Each answer names an item of knowledge and a repair beside the damage."@en ;
    skos:note "A graded catalogue of failure conditions for one rite, given in the transmission's own voice, with the damage named in each case and rising from one person to the whole household. Nothing else this holding has read states the consequences of a single kind of mishap in five degrees."@en ;
    skos:note "The damage falls on the eldest son, on the master of the house, on the cattle and on the line — never on the officiant who is speaking. Where the sacrificer is himself the offerer the second case reaches him, and it is the only one of the five that does."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:VaidikaAgnihotra ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XI,5,3,8-12 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, pp. 82-85" .

pmc:VaidikaJnanenaJitam a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "By dint of knowledge I myself have prevailed"@en, "Kraft des Wissens habe ich selbst obsiegt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The clause attached to every one of the five cases, in the same words each time. The damage is stated for him who would not know this; and the man questioned adds of himself: but by dint of knowledge I myself have prevailed. The knowledge in each case is an identification of one breath with another — the breath of the mouth has entered the upward breathing, and so on — and the repair is an offering moved to a different fire."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the finding of the passage and it is the reason the tradition was worth taking off the register. The same mishap, the same fire, the same hour: the eldest son dies or he does not, and what decides it is what the offerer knows. No observation of the mishap distinguishes the two cases, and the knowledge is not a skill anybody could see exercised — it is an equivalence held in the mind and reported only by the man who holds it."@en ;
    skos:note "It is not a later apology either. The clause is not attached to a complaint that the rite failed; it stands in the first statement of the danger, in the mouth of the authority who states it, as part of what makes him an authority. The excusing condition and the threat are the same sentence."@en ;
    skos:note "What the passage does supply against itself, and it is unusual: it says outright that the knowledge is rare. The exchange is a contest in which one party is repeatedly told that this much they know in common, until at the fifth case Śauceya answers that he did not know this. See pmc:VaidikaMurdhanvyapatana for what follows."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XI,5,3,8-12 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, pp. 82-85" .

pmc:VaidikaMurdhanvyapatana a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "If thou hadst not spoken thus, thy head would have flown off"@en, "Hättest du nicht so gesprochen, wäre dir das Haupt abgeflogen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The close of the contest. Śauceya, having admitted that he did not know the fifth answer, says: here are logs for fuel, I will become thy pupil. Uddālaka replies: if thou hadst not spoken thus, thy head would have flown off; come, enter as my pupil. He then initiates him and teaches him that pain-conquering utterance, Truth."@en ;
    skos:note "A penalty for asking beyond one's own knowledge, stated after the fact and cancelled by the submission that was offered before it was announced. The one act that averts it is acknowledging the other man as teacher, so the sanction protects the order of the school and not the rite."@en ;
    skos:note "The same sanction is stated twice more in the corpus, and neither place was read at the wording in this run: at Śatapatha XI,4,1,9, to which Eggeling's note refers, and at XIV,6,9,28, where Vidagdha Śākalya's head falls off. The addresses are recorded here so the next run does not have to find them again."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:VaidikaAgnihotra ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XI,5,3,13 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, p. 85" .

#
# The expiation, and its silence
#

pmp:VaidikaPrayascitta a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "Prāyaścitta, the repair of a mishap in the rite"@en, "Prāyaścitta, die Wiedergutmachung eines Zwischenfalls im Ritus"@de ;
    skos:definition "The prescribed repair for a mishap during the performance. The transmission sets it out case by case in a standing form: they also say, if such and such were to happen, what rite and what expiation would there be in that case? — followed by the act to be done, and closed with: this, then, is the rite performed in that case. The cases read include a team or persons passing between the fires, unclean animals passing between them, milk spilt at the milking and milk spilt after ladling, the cow giving blood instead of milk, the vessel overturning or breaking, the fire going out after the offering, the loss of the householder's fire, the fires touching one another or an unclean fire, and the sun rising or setting before the offering is carried out."@en ;
    skos:note "Some twenty-two of these stood in the four chapters read, the great majority in the standing form quoted above. What they show as a set is a corpus that has thought about its own accidents in detail; what they do not show is entered at pmc:VaidikaPrayascittaSarvam."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XII,4,1 to XII,4,4 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, pp. 177-196" .

pmc:VaidikaPrayascittaSarvam a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "The expiation is said to work, in every case and without a sign"@en, "Die Sühne wirkt, in jedem Falle und ohne Zeichen"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:VaidikaPrayascitta ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:VaidikaPrayascittaEffectus ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    skos:note "Entered with its weakness stated, because the weakness is the finding. Twenty-two enumerated mishaps, a prescribed act for each, one general formula that covers whatever is not enumerated — the three utterances bhūr bhuvaḥ svar, of which the text says that these utterances are all-expiatory and that he thus makes expiation with all this universe — and one general assurance: no hurt and no harm of any kind befalls where that expiation is made. In four chapters the run found no sign by which a completed expiation is known to have taken effect, no interval within which the averted harm would otherwise have come, and no case in which an expiation failed. The search is at examinations/searches-vedic.ttl."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XII,4,1,8 and XII,4,4 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, pp. 180 and 196" .

pmc:VaidikaPrayascittaEffectus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "No hurt and no harm of any kind befalls where that expiation is made"@en, "Kein Schaden und kein Übel irgendwelcher Art trifft den, bei dem jene Sühne vollzogen wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The effect claimed for the repair, in the transmission's own closing words to the chapter on the sun rising or setting over an uncarried offering: the import is the same as before, and verily, no hurt and no harm of any kind befalls where that expiation is made."@en ;
    skos:note "The generality is in the sentence and not in the reading of it. It does not say that the particular harm of the particular mishap is averted; it says that no harm of any kind befalls. A claim of that shape has no outcome left over that could count against it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XII,4,4 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, p. 196" .

pmc:VaidikaSkannamPrajananam a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "The spilt milk read as a sign of increase"@en, "Die verschüttete Milch als Zeichen der Vermehrung gelesen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The repair for spilling turns the mishap into a promise. He touches the spilt milk with the words: it hath been shed, it hath been implanted, birth hath ensued. The exegesis follows: this earth is a womb and the milk is seed, he thus implants seed in that womb, and forthwith that shed seed of him who so knows this is born forth. The paragraph ends by telling him what to conclude — let him therefore know that abundant production has accrued unto him, that he will be multiplied in offspring and cattle, and that he will become more prosperous."@en ;
    skos:note "The mishap and its opposite are now both favourable. Milk that stays in the vessel is a correct performance; milk that goes on the ground is abundant production accruing to the performer. The instruction is explicit that this is what he is to know from the event, so nothing about the event remains that could tell him the day went badly."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands two paragraphs from the general assurance at pmc:VaidikaPrayascittaEffectus and does a different job. There the repair removes the harm; here the mishap is reclassified. Both are in the same chapter and neither is presented as an exception."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XII,4,1,7 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, p. 180" .

pmc:VaidikaRudhiradoha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "The cow that gives blood, and the man the evil is fastened on"@en, "Die Kuh, die Blut gibt, und der Mann, dem das Übel angeheftet wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "One of the enumerated mishaps, with a repair that moves the harm to a named person. If the Agnihotra cow were to milk blood, he says Disperse, has the southern fire enclosed, boils the blood on it and offers it silently and in an undefined way, because Prajāpati is undefined and the undefined means everything, so that he makes atonement with everything. At this offering he should give that cow to a Brahmaṇa whom he does not intend to visit; for she who milks blood milks it after perceiving the sacrificer's suffering and evil, and he thus fastens that suffering and evil upon this Brahmaṇa. He then offers with what other milk he can procure."@en ;
    skos:note "The mishap is treated as a symptom and not as an accident: the cow is said to have perceived an evil that was already in the sacrificer. The repair does not remove the evil, it transfers it, and the instruction to choose a Brahmaṇa he does not intend to visit says plainly that the recipient is not to be seen again. This is the most explicit transfer of a ritual penalty to an uninvolved third party that this holding has read."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XII,4,2,1 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, p. 183" .

#
# What knowing is said to obtain
#

pmp:VaidikaSvadhyaya a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "Svādhyāya, the daily recitation of one's own lesson"@en, "Svādhyāya, die tägliche Rezitation des eigenen Pensums"@de ;
    skos:definition "The daily study and teaching of the Veda, which the transmission calls a sacrifice to the Brahman and sets out in the same terms as a fire-rite: it has its own oblations and its own vaṣaṭ calls, and it is to be performed whether or not any fire is lit."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XI,5,6,9 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, p. 99" .

pmc:VaidikaSvadhyayaPunarmrtyujaya a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "The daily recitation frees from the death that comes again"@en, "Die tägliche Rezitation befreit vom wiederkehrenden Tod"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:VaidikaSvadhyaya ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:VaidikaPunarmrtyu ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    skos:note "The same outcome the fire-altar is said to obtain, obtained here by recitation alone, with an occasion attached: the four vaṣaṭ calls of this sacrifice are the wind blowing, the lightning, the thunder and the rumbling, and he who knows this should study when they occur so as not to lose them. And a floor is set under it — should he be altogether unable to study, let him read a single divine word, and thus he is not shut out from beings."@en ;
    skos:note "Worth holding beside pmc:VaidikaAgnicayanaAmrtatva rather than merged with it. Two operations of very different cost are credited with the same result in the same corpus, and one of them can be discharged by one word on a day when nothing else is possible."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XI,5,6,9 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, p. 99" .

pmc:VaidikaTulamana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "He who knows this mounts the balance in this world"@en, "Wer dies weiß, besteigt die Waage schon in dieser Welt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The right edge of the altar-ground is the balance. Whatever good deed a man does is inside the altar-ground and whatever evil deed outside it; let him therefore sit down touching the right edge. For they place him on the balance in the world beyond, and whichever of the two rises he will follow, whether good or evil. And whosoever knows this mounts the balance even in this world and escapes being placed on the balance in the world beyond, for his good deed rises and not his evil deed."@en ;
    skos:note "A weighing whose outcome is fixed in advance for whoever knows how to sit. The passage states the general rule — the rising pan decides — and in the same breath names a condition under which the outcome is not in doubt. Eggeling's note refers this to the Indian ordeal literature, which was not opened here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XI,2,7,33 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, pp. 45-46" .

pmc:VaidikaPranitavimocana a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "The water is poured out, or the sacrifice injures the sacrificer"@en, "Das Wasser wird ausgegossen, sonst verletzt das Opfer den Opferherrn"@de ;
    skos:definition "At the close of the new and full-moon offering the priest walks round to the south and pours out the praṇītā-water. The reason is stated as a mechanism: when he spreads the sacrifice he yokes it; but were he not to pour out the water, the sacrifice, being unyoked, would in moving backward injure the sacrificer. In this way, however, the sacrifice does not injure the sacrificer."@en ;
    skos:note "The plainest statement in the corpus of the shape this project looks for in a prerequisite: an act, an omission, and a named consequence of the omission, all in one sentence and with the consequence explained rather than asserted. The sacrifice is a yoked team; what is not unyoked at the end runs back over the man who drove it."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:VaidikaDarsapurnamasa ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:VaidikaPranitavisarjanam ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "But were he not to pour out the praṇītā-water, the sacrifice, being unyoked, would, in moving backward, injure the sacrificer."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa I,9,2,32 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 12, Oxford 1882, p. 265" .

#
# Whether there is a fault at all: the disagreement the Kauṣītaki carries
#

pmc:VaidikaDosanivedananisedhah a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KausitakiBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "The priest in the Sadas must not call attention to a flaw"@en, "Der Priester im Sadas soll auf einen Fehler nicht aufmerksam machen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Prāgāhi holds that once the concluding call of the invitatory and offering verses has been made, a flaw noticed after that point has been passed over. Paiṅgya draws the rule from it: therefore the priest in the Sadas should not call attention to a flaw passed over at this time — he knocks into a pillar or falls into a pit or is put into one or perishes. Āruṇi holds that if the flaw is pointed out there must not be a repetition of what has been done; Śvetaketu, that there is loss of a secondary element. Paiṅgya repeats his rule twice more and adds: it is a tribulation to the sacrifice if the priest in the Sadas calls attention to a flaw passed over."@en ;
    skos:note "A prohibition on reporting an error, with a consequence named for the reporter and not for the error. The office described is the one office at the rite whose business is to watch it, and the rule addressed to him is to keep quiet."@en ;
    skos:note "Worth keeping apart from the transmissions that forbid a testing question. Here nothing is being tested: the flaw is agreed to have occurred, and what is forbidden is saying so. The consequence — a pillar, a pit, perishing — falls on the man who speaks, which is the reverse of the arrangement everywhere else in this file, where the fault of one man is paid for by another."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:VaidikaNivid, pmp:VaidikaVasatkara ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Kauṣītaki Brāhmaṇa xxvi,4, in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, pp. 497-498" .

pmc:VaidikaDosavivadah a pm:Disputing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KausitakiBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "Whether a flaw in the recitation has to be repaired, or whether there is none"@en, "Ob ein Fehler in der Rezitation zu heilen ist, oder ob es keinen gibt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The corpus stages the question and names the parties. Daivodāsi Pratardana comes to a sacrificial session of the Naimiṣīyas and asks: if the priest in the Sadas should call attention to a flaw passed over, or any one of the priests should note it, how would you remove the flaw? They were silent. Their Brahman priest Alikayu Vācaspata says he does not know and will ask Jātūkarṇya, the aged teacher of those formerly. Jātūkarṇya answers that the mantra should be recited again, and, asked how much, that so much as is erroneous only need be repeated — a verse, or half verse, or quarter verse, or word, or letter. But Kauṣītaki used to say: he should not repeat the mantra, nor offer a libation; there is no flaw — for whatever flaw the Hotṛs make in the sacrifice through inattention, all that Agni as divine Hotṛ makes flawless."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the most valuable thing the run read, and what makes it so is that both positions are held by named authorities inside one transmission. On one side the fault is real and repairable to the letter, which is as fine-grained a correction rule as any corpus in this holding states. On the other side there is no fault, because a god repairs whatever inattention produces. The second position is the immunising clause stated as doctrine and attributed by name, not reconstructed by an outsider."@en ;
    skos:note "What the two positions do to the exactness rule is the point. The whole apparatus entered above — the empty vaṣaṭ, the three faults of the Nivid, the misspoken formula — presupposes that an error has an effect. Kauṣītaki's sentence removes the effect of every error made through inattention, and with it every occasion on which the rule could be seen to hold or fail. The corpus therefore carries the strictest form of the exactness claim and its complete cancellation, two sections apart."@en ;
    skos:note "Keith notes that this passage has no parallel in the Aitareya and none in the Śatapatha, and that the exact sense of Prāgāhi's sentence is uncertain. The uncertainty is about when the flaw counts as passed over and not about the two answers, which are plain."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:VaidikaNividdosah ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Kauṣītaki Brāhmaṇa xxvi,5, the position of Jātūkarṇya, in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, p. 498", "Kauṣītaki Brāhmaṇa xxvi,5, the position of Kauṣītaki, in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, p. 498" .

pmc:VaidikaSamkhyapurnata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "The completeness of the count"@en, "Die Vollständigkeit der Zahl"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition Prajāpati names and the gods then meet: three hundred and sixty enclosing-stones, three hundred and sixty bricks laid with formulas and thirty-six more, and ten thousand eight hundred filling bricks. The transmission reads each class against a division of the year — the stones for the nights, the formula-bricks for the days, half-moons, months and seasons, the filling bricks for the hours — and distributes the stones by hearth: twenty-one round the householder's fire, seventy-eight round the subsidiary hearths, two hundred and sixty-one round the offering fire."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as its own node because it is the one condition in this file that an onlooker could check while the work was going on. Everything else the corpus makes efficacy depend on is either a state of mind or an event after death."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa X,4,3,8 and X,4,3,12-13 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 43, Oxford 1897, pp. 357-358" .

pmp:VaidikaDarsapurnamasa a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "The new and full-moon offering"@en, "Das Neu- und Vollmondopfer"@de ;
    skos:definition "The paired offerings at the two turns of the moon, which the transmission treats as the model performance and to which it attaches both the legend of the misspoken formula and the closing act of unyoking. The exegesis states what the full-moon offering is worth to the performer in the same breath as the legend: he who, knowing this, performs it in this wise attains to the same state of prosperity as Indra did."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa I,6,3,15 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 12, Oxford 1882, p. 167" .

pmc:VaidikaPranitavisarjanam a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "The pouring out of the praṇītā-water as the unyoking"@en, "Das Ausgießen des Praṇītā-Wassers als das Ausspannen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The closing act. Having walked round from the north of the offering fire to the south, he pours the praṇītā-water out on the altar, with the words: who unyokes thee? he unyokes thee! for whom does he unyoke thee? for him he unyokes thee! for plenty! He pours it out with the same vessel with which he brought it forward, because the team is unyoked with the same tie by which it is yoked."@en ;
    skos:note "The identification is what carries the prescription: spreading the sacrifice is yoking it, and the vessel is the yoke-tie. The prohibition at pmc:VaidikaPranitavimocana follows from the image and is stated as its consequence."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa I,9,2,32-33 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 12, Oxford 1882, p. 265" .

#
# The one case the corpus reports of the threatened harm arriving
#

pmc:VaidikaAsadhimrtyu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    rdfs:label "They did so for Aṣāḍhi Sauśromateya, and quickly he died after that"@en, "Man tat es so für Aṣāḍhi Sauśromateya, und bald darauf starb er"@de ;
    skos:definition "The victims' heads that go into the fire-altar are to come from an animal sacrifice performed for the purpose. The chapter states the rule by naming the shortcut and its price: some, having obtained those heads in that way — somehow or other, by buying or begging them, as the commentator glosses it — put them on the altar, thinking that either way they are animals; but they who do this become mortal carcases, for unpropitiated are those heads of theirs. Then the case: in this way, indeed, they did put them on for Aṣāḍhi Sauśromateya; but quickly indeed he died after that. The chapter goes on to reject gold heads as false bricks and earthen ones as a substitute for the man who knows neither the practice nor the theory."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the only place in the five volumes read where the corpus reports that a stated consequence of a ritual fault actually arrived, and it came out of a search rather than a reading: the whole run was looking for such a case and had provisionally concluded there was none. The search and its terms stand at examinations/searches-vedic.ttl."@en ;
    skos:note "What the case is worth, stated exactly. It names a man, it names the fault, it names the outcome, and it gives an interval — quickly, and nothing more precise. It reports one instance and no others, gives no number of men who used bought heads and lived, and is told inside the chapter that forbids the shortcut. Nothing here is a count; it is the transmission's own confirming anecdote, which is a different thing and the reason this holding keeps a class for cases and a class for claims."@en ;
    skos:note "Who bore it is worth noting beside the rest. The heads were put on for Aṣāḍhi, by others; the sentence is in the plural for the doing and the singular for the dying. The fault is the officiants', the death is the patron's, which is the arrangement generalizations/displaced-penalties.ttl describes, here with the consequence reported as having been seen to arrive."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa VI,2,1,37 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 41, Oxford 1894, pp. 170-171" .
